# Cryptographic test fixtures are bytes, not text.
#
# A clearsigned OpenPGP signature is a TEXT-mode signature and canonicalises
# line endings, so today's fixtures verify whether git checks them out with
# LF or CRLF -- that was measured, not assumed. A detached binary signature
# would not, and the failure would be a signature that verifies on Linux and
# does not on Windows, which reads as a code bug for as long as it takes
# somebody to think of line endings.
#
# Marking them binary costs nothing and removes the class.
apps/api/tests/fixtures/pgp/** -text

# Migrations are executed as-is against Postgres. Nothing here depends on
# line endings, but a diff that shows a whole file changed because a
# checkout rewrote it makes review harder for no benefit.
*.sql text eol=lf

# Shell scripts MUST keep LF, and this one is not cosmetic.
#
# `#!/usr/bin/env bash\r` is not a shebang any kernel recognises. A release
# installer authored on Windows and checked out with CRLF fails on the
# Linux box it was written for with:
#
#     /usr/bin/env: 'bash\r': No such file or directory
#
# which names neither the file nor line endings, and reads as a corrupt
# download. The whole point of shipping install.sh is that it works on a
# machine nobody has prepared, so it cannot depend on the person who
# cloned it having configured core.autocrlf correctly.
*.sh text eol=lf

# The packaged release is a BUILD ARTIFACT that lives in the repo, so
# `git archive` would put the previous copy inside the new one -- and the
# next regeneration would nest that, doubling the file every time.
#
# It has not happened yet only because the zip was first built before it
# was itself committed. Excluding it from the export closes the trap
# before somebody runs package_release.ps1 twice and wonders why the
# download is 5MB.
# (quoted: gitattributes splits on whitespace, and this path has spaces --
# unquoted it parsed as three patterns and silently did nothing.)
"NocTORnal - Alpha Release.zip" export-ignore
